title: sigma_convergence
---



```python
sigma_convergence(
    data,
    variable,
    *,
    first=None,
    last=None,
    vcov='hetero',
    title=None,
    ref=None,
)
```

Sigma-convergence: does the dispersion of a variable across units fall over time?

Measures the dispersion of the logarithm of ``variable`` in every year, on the units
observed in all of them, and estimates its trend. A negative trend is convergence.

## Parameters {.doc-section .doc-section-parameters}

| Name     | Type        | Description                                                                                          | Default    |
|----------|-------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------|
| data     |             | A dataset id, or a panel with ``geo_id`` and ``year``, e.g. from ``load``                            | _required_ |
| variable | str         | A numeric, strictly positive column in levels (its logarithm is taken here)                          | _required_ |
| first    |             | The first and last year to use (default: every year with data). The trend needs at least three years | `None`     |
| last     |             | The first and last year to use (default: every year with data). The trend needs at least three years | `None`     |
| vcov     | str         | Standard errors of the trend: ``"hetero"`` (robust, HC1) or ``"iid"``                                | `'hetero'` |
| title    | str \| None | The figure title                                                                                     | `None`     |

## Returns {.doc-section .doc-section-returns}

| Name   | Type          | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |
|--------|---------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|        | result object | A result with ``.std_slope`` (the trend of the log standard deviation per year: negative when dispersion falls), ``.std_se``, ``.std_pvalue`` and ``.std_r2``, the same for the Gini index (``.gini_slope``...) and the coefficient of variation (``.cv_slope``...), ``.n_units``, ``.n_periods``, ``.df`` (one row per year: ``n_units``, ``mean``, ``std``, ``gini`` and ``cv`` of the logarithm), ``.fig`` (dispersion over time with the fitted trends), ``.gt`` and ``.summary`` (one row per measure), ``.notes`` (among them the units left out for lack of data) and ``.interpret()``. |

## Example {.doc-section .doc-section-example}

```python
!pip install -q geo4bolivia
import geo4bolivia as geo

res = geo.sigma_convergence("pop", "pop")            # 20 years, 333 units
print(res.interpret())
res.fig
```


::: {#c8a0a941 .cell execution_count=1}

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display .cell-output-markdown}
Across 333 units and 20 years, the cross-sectional dispersion of **Population** (standard deviation of its log) narrowed by about 0.196% per year (statistically significant at the 1% level).
A negative log-dispersion trend is σ-convergence: the units are becoming more alike.
All estimated measures (standard deviation, Gini, coefficient of variation) trend downward, so the narrowing is not an artifact of one dispersion metric.

_These are associations, not causal effects. A causal reading needs a research design._
:::

